  {
    "pkattachmentId": 37780,
    "bookid": 35,
    "chapterId": 23,
    "fileId": 14129,
    "type": "Table",
    "diagnosis": "",
    "title": "<p>Distinction of minimally invasive adenocarcinoma from lepidic-predominant adenocarcinoma</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "Wendy A. Cooper",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoTableGridLight\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; border: none; \" border=\"0\" width=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\" page-break-inside: avoid; height: 3.5pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 184.3pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"246\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"page-break-after: avoid;\"><strong style=\"\">Diagnostic feature</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 245.3pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"327\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center; page-break-after: avoid;\" align=\"center\"><strong style=\"\">Minimally invasive adenocarcinoma</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 273.7pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"365\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center; page-break-after: avoid;\" align=\"center\"><strong style=\"\">Lepidic-predominant adenocarcinoma</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\" page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 184.3pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"246\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"\">Size of total tumour</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 245.3pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"327\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">Lepidic-predominant tumour &le;&nbsp;30&nbsp;mm</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 273.7pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"365\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">Lepidic-predominant tumour of any size <em style=\"\">or</em></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">Tumour that otherwise meets the criteria for minimally invasive adenocarcinoma but is &gt;&nbsp;30&nbsp;mm</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\" page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 184.3pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"246\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"\">Size of invasive focus</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 245.3pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"327\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">&le;&nbsp;5&nbsp;mm</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 273.7pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"365\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">&gt;&nbsp;5&nbsp;mm</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\" page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 184.3pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"246\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"\">Invasion of visceral pleura or blood vessels or lymphatic vessels</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 245.3pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"327\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">Absent</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 273.7pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"365\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">May be present</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\" page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 184.3pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"246\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"\">Tumour necrosis</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 245.3pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"327\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">Absent</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 273.7pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"365\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">May be present</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\" page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 184.3pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"246\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"\">Spread through airspaces</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 245.3pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"327\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">Absent</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 273.7pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"365\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">May be present</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>",
    "imageUrl": null,
    "thumbnail": null
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 37781,
    "bookid": 35,
    "chapterId": 23,
    "fileId": 14130,
    "type": "Table",
    "diagnosis": "",
    "title": "<p>Features to help distinguish lepidic pattern of adenocarcinoma from invasive adenocarcinoma</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "Wendy A. Cooper",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoTableGridLight\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; border: none; \" border=\"0\" width=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\" page-break-inside: avoid; height: 3.5pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 159.8pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"213\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"page-break-after: avoid;\"><strong style=\"\">Diagnostic feature</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 242.15pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"323\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center; page-break-after: avoid;\" align=\"center\"><strong style=\"\">Lepidic pattern of <span style=\"\">adenocarcinoma<sup>a</sup></span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 217.1pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"289\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center; page-break-after: avoid;\" align=\"center\"><strong style=\"\">Invasive adenocarcinoma</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\" page-break-inside: avoid; height: 3.5pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 159.8pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"213\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"page-break-after: avoid;\"><strong style=\"\">Histological pattern</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 242.15pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"323\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center; page-break-after: avoid;\" align=\"center\">Lepidic</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 217.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"289\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center; page-break-after: avoid;\" align=\"center\">Acinar, papillary, micropapillary, or solid</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\" page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 159.8pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"213\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"\">Myofibroblastic stroma</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 242.15pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"323\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">Absent</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 217.1pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"289\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">May be present</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\" page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 159.8pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"213\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"\">Intra-alveolar macrophages</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 242.15pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"323\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">May be present</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 217.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"289\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">Usually absent</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\" page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 159.8pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"213\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"\">Alveolar architecture</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 242.15pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"323\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">Preserved</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 217.1pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"289\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\"><span style=\"\">Lost</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><sup><span style=\"\">a</span></sup><span style=\"\">This includes non-mucinous adenocarcinoma in situ or lepidic pattern of adenocarcinoma within a minimally invasive adenocarcinoma or an invasive non-mucinous adenocarcinoma</span>.</p>",
    "imageUrl": null,
    "thumbnail": null
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 37806,
    "bookid": 35,
    "chapterId": 23,
    "fileId": 14795,
    "type": "Table",
    "diagnosis": "",
    "title": "<p>Proposed grading of resected early-stage invasive non-mucinous lung adenocarcinoma</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "Wendy A. Cooper",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoTableGridLight\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; border: none;\" border=\"0\" width=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 3.5pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 49.5pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"66\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center; page-break-after: avoid;\" align=\"center\"><strong>Grade</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 134.8pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"180\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center; page-break-after: avoid;\" align=\"center\"><strong>Differentiation</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 519.0pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"692\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"page-break-after: avoid;\"><strong>Patterns</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 49.5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"66\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\"><strong>1</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 134.8pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"180\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">Well-differentiated</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 519.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"692\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Lepidic-predominant with no or &lt;&nbsp;20% high-grade pattern</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 49.5pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"66\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\"><strong>2</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 134.8pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"180\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">Moderately differentiated</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 519.0pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"692\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Acinar or papillary-predominant with no or &lt;&nbsp;20% high-grade pattern</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 49.5pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"66\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\"><strong>3</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 134.8pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"180\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">Poorly differentiated</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 519.0pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"692\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Any tumour with &ge;&nbsp;20% high-grade pattern (solid, micropapillary, cribriform, or complex glandular pattern<sup>a</sup>)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><sup>a</sup>Fused glands or single cells infiltrating in a desmoplastic stroma.</p>",
    "imageUrl": null,
    "thumbnail": null
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 37812,
    "bookid": 35,
    "chapterId": 23,
    "fileId": 15061,
    "type": "Table",
    "diagnosis": "",
    "title": "<p>Characteristics of <em>EGFR</em> mutations in lung adenocarcinoma</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "",
    "copyright": "Yatabe Y.",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoTableGridLight\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3; border-collapse: collapse; border: none;\" border=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"height: 17.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 678.35pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 17.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"708\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>The mutations occur in the kinase domain of the receptor tyrosine kinase, and they lead to constitutive activation of downstream signalling without ligand binding.</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>Although females and never-smokers are preferentially affected, the biological basis for these associations is not well understood. However, every new case of advanced adenocarcinoma should be tested for the presence of <em>EGFR</em> mutations.</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>The two most common mutations&nbsp;&ndash; the point mutation at codon&nbsp;858 (p.L858R) and the in-frame deletions in exon&nbsp;19&nbsp;&ndash; account for &gt;&nbsp;90% of cases, although many other mutations (e.g. mutations at codon&nbsp;G719 and in-frame insertions in exon&nbsp;20) are reported in the literature.</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>These <em>EGFR</em> mutations are highly suggestive of lung adenocarcinoma. Among adenocarcinomas, <em>EGFR</em> mutations are frequently detected in cases with lepidic and papillary growth, and they are associated with TTF1 positivity.</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>Genetic alterations of other major lung cancer driver genes, such as <em>KRAS</em>, <em>ALK</em>, <em>ROS1</em>, <em>BRAF</em>, <em>RET</em>, and <em>ERBB2</em>, are mutually exclusive with <em>EGFR</em> mutations, presumably because these all converge on the same intracellular signalling pathways, and a single impairment in these pathways is sufficient to drive tumour formation.</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>Rare families with germline <em>EGFR</em> mutations (p.R776G, p.R776H, p.T790M, p.V843I, and p.P848L) have higher risk of lung adenocarcinoma, which can be multifocal, particularly in the case of germline <em>EGFR</em> p.T790M mutations.</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><em>EGFR</em> mutations in lung adenocarcinoma show ethnic differences, with prevalence ranges of 10&ndash;15% in people of European descent and 30&ndash;40% in Asians.</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><em>EGFR</em> mutation is a prognostic factor as well as a factor predictive of response to first- and second-generation EGFR tyrosine kinase inhibitor treatment.</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><em>EGFR</em> mutations in exon&nbsp;20, in-frame insertions, and (rarely) p.T790M mutations are associated with primary resistance to EGFR tyrosine kinase inhibitors, and acquisition of an additional p.T790M mutation is the most common cause of secondary resistance to EGFR tyrosine kinase inhibitors.</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>",
    "imageUrl": null,
    "thumbnail": null
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 37813,
    "bookid": 35,
    "chapterId": 23,
    "fileId": 15062,
    "type": "Table",
    "diagnosis": "",
    "title": "<p>Characteristics of <em>ALK</em> gene fusions in lung cancer</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "",
    "copyright": "Yatabe Y",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoTableGridLight\" style=\"background: #DAEEF3; border-collapse: collapse; border: none;\" border=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"height: 17.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 678.35pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 17.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"708\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><em>ALK</em> fusions are detected in various tumours of the lung, thyroid, oesophagus, soft tissue, kidney, colorectum, and bladder, and the fusion partner genes vary among the tumour types. <em>EML4</em>-<em>ALK</em> fusion is most frequently and almost exclusively found in carcinomas of the lung.</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><em>ALK</em> rearrangement in lung cancer is strongly associated with adenocarcinoma histology, in particular with acinar and/or solid growth pattern, or with cellular features of signet-ring cell carcinoma.</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><em>EML4</em>-<em>ALK</em> fusion accounts for &gt;&nbsp;90% of <em>ALK</em> rearrangements in lung adenocarcinomas. Other (less common) <em>ALK</em> fusion partners include <em>KIF5B</em>, <em>KLC1</em>, <em>TFG</em>, and others.</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>Like <em>EGFR</em> mutations, <em>ALK</em> rearrangement is frequent in never-smokers, but it is less associated with female sex.</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>The median age of patients with ALK-positive lung cancer is about 10&nbsp;years younger than that of patients with ALK-negative cancer.</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span>ALK-positive lung adenocarcinoma constitutes 4&ndash;5% of non-small cell lung carcinomas, and ethnic differences have not been reported, unlike with <em>EGFR</em> mutations.</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><em>ALK</em> rearrangement is predictive of response to ALK inhibitor treatment, but it is not a prognostic factor.</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>",
    "imageUrl": null,
    "thumbnail": null
  },
  {
